+\begin{proof}
+We first justify differentiation with the unbounded current observable.
+Fix $T>0$ and $0\le\eps\le1$. By~\eqref{eq:propagator_H2}, the
+propagators are uniformly bounded on $H^2_{\rm per}$ for
+$0\le s\le t\le T$ and $k\in\mathcal B$.
+The occupied unit vectors also have uniformly bounded $H^2_{\rm per}$
+norms, since their energies are bounded.
+
+For such a vector $u$, set
+$\psi_\eps(t)=U_{\eps,k}(t)u$ and $\psi_0(t)=e^{-itH_k}u$.
+Since
+\[
+H_{k+\eps se_\beta}-H_k
+=\eps s\partial_\beta H_k+\frac{\eps^2s^2|e_\beta|^2}{2},
+\]
+Duhamel's formula gives
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:duhamel_current}
+\psi_\eps(t)-\psi_0(t)
+=-i\int_0^t e^{-i(t-s)H_k}
+\left(\eps s\partial_\beta H_k+\frac{\eps^2s^2|e_\beta|^2}{2}\right)
+\psi_\eps(s)\,ds.
+\end{equation}
+The difference is $O_T(\eps)$ in $L^2_{\rm per}$ and bounded in
+$H^2_{\rm per}$, hence $O_T(\sqrt\eps)$ in $H^1_{\rm per}$ by
+interpolation. Dividing~\eqref{eq:duhamel_current} by $\eps$ therefore
+gives, in $L^2_{\rm per}$,
+\[
+\frac{\psi_\eps(t)-\psi_0(t)}{\eps}
+\longrightarrow-i\int_0^t s\,e^{-i(t-s)H_k}
+\partial_\beta H_ke^{-isH_k}u\,ds.
+\]
+All these bounds and the convergence are uniform in $k$, in occupied
+unit vectors $u$, and in $0\le t\le T$.
+
+Put $w=\psi_\eps-\psi_0$. The identity
+\[
+\langle\psi_\eps,\partial_\alpha H_k\psi_\eps\rangle
+-\langle\psi_0,\partial_\alpha H_k\psi_0\rangle
+=2\operatorname{Re}\langle w,\partial_\alpha H_k\psi_0\rangle
++\langle w,\partial_\alpha H_kw\rangle
+\]
+has last term $O_T(\eps^{3/2})$. Thus the current expectations are
+differentiable, with a uniform $o_T(\eps)$ remainder. Summing over the
+$N$ occupied vectors gives the density-matrix derivative
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:density_derivative}
+\left.\frac{d}{d\eps}\widetilde\gamma_{\eps,k}(t)\right|_{\eps=0}
+=-i\int_0^t s\,e^{-i(t-s)L_k}[\partial_\beta H_k,P_k]\,ds.
+\end{equation}
+This formula also holds when paired with $\partial_\alpha H_k$:
+$\partial_\beta H_ku\in H^1_{\rm per}$, and the free propagator
+preserves this form domain.
+
+We now use~\eqref{eq:Pprime}. Since
+$[\partial_\beta H_k,P_k]=-L_k\partial_\beta P_k$ and
+$\partial_\beta P_k$ is off-diagonal, integration by parts gives
+\[
+-i\int_0^t s\,e^{-i(t-s)L_k}[\partial_\beta H_k,P_k]\,ds
+=t\partial_\beta P_k-i(e^{-itL_k}-1)L_k^+\partial_\beta P_k.
+\]
+By the Hellmann--Feynman theorem,
+\begin{align*}
+\partial_\alpha\Tr(H_kP_k)&=\Tr(\partial_\alpha H_kP_k),\\
+\partial_\alpha\partial_\beta\Tr(H_kP_k)
+&=N e_\alpha\cdot e_\beta
++\Tr(\partial_\alpha H_k\partial_\beta P_k).
+\end{align*}
+Together with
+$\partial_\alpha H_{k+\eps te_\beta}
+=\partial_\alpha H_k+\eps t e_\alpha\cdot e_\beta$,
+these identities prove~\eqref{eq:response_expansion}.
+The uniform bounds justify the stated limit interchanges, and
+Proposition~\ref{prop:null_current} removes the equilibrium current.
 \end{proof}
 
 
