+\section{Proof of the conductivity formula}\label{sec:semimet}
+
+We prove here Theorem~\ref{theo:semi-metal}. We first take the limit
+$\eps\to0^+$ in Proposition~\ref{prop:response}, and cancel the term
+proportional to $t$. After averaging in time, only the two crossing
+bands near each Dirac point contribute to the limit. We construct
+their reduced Hamiltonian and compare it with the Dirac Hamiltonian,
+for which we can explicitly compute the limiting current.
+
+\subsection{Cancellation of the term linear in time}
+\label{sec:band_cancellation}
+
+For $n\le N<m$, write
+\begin{align}\label{eq:transition_data}
+\Delta_{nm,k}&=\lambda_{m,k}-\lambda_{n,k},\nonumber\\
+z_{nm,k}&=\langle u_{n,k},\partial_\beta H_ku_{m,k}\rangle
+\langle u_{m,k},\partial_\alpha H_ku_{n,k}\rangle.
+\end{align}
+The finite number of occupied states, the uniform bounds on
+$\partial_\alpha H_ku_{n,k}$ and $\partial_\beta H_ku_{n,k}$,
+and Cauchy--Schwarz give
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:transition_bound}
+\sum_{n\le N<m}|z_{nm,k}|\le C.
+\end{equation}
+This controls the infinitely many unoccupied bands. Sums over complete
+eigenspaces are independent of the choice of basis at internal band crossings.
+
+Let $F(k)=\Tr(H_kP_k)$. The spectral derivative~\eqref{eq:Pprime}
+and the eigenbasis representation~\eqref{eq:explicit_inverse_liouvillian}
+give
+\[
+\partial_\alpha\partial_\beta F(k)
+=N e_\alpha\cdot e_\beta
+-2\sum_{n\le N<m}\frac{\operatorname{Re}z_{nm,k}}{\Delta_{nm,k}}.
+\]
+The gap is bounded below away from the crossings and is comparable
+to $|k-k_i|$ near $k_i$. Thus this derivative is integrable.
+Applying Stokes formula on the momentum torus with disks of radius $r$
+removed gives
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:filled_band_cancellation}
+\int_{\mathcal B}\partial_\alpha\partial_\beta F(k)\,dk=0.
+\end{equation}
+Indeed, $\partial_\alpha F(k)=\Tr(\partial_\alpha H_kP_k)$ is bounded,
+so the extra boundary integrals are $O(r)$ and vanish as $r\to0$.
+
+For the remaining term of~\eqref{eq:response_expansion}, we use
+\begin{align*}
+&\Tr\left(\partial_\alpha H_k(e^{-itL_k}-1)L_k^+\partial_\beta P_k\right)\\
+&\qquad=\sum_{n\le N<m}(e^{it\Delta_{nm,k}}-1)
+\frac{z_{nm,k}}{\Delta_{nm,k}^2}-{\rm c.c.}
+\end{align*}
+Time reversal leaves the gaps unchanged and conjugates the products
+$z_{nm,k}$. Their imaginary part therefore integrates to zero.
+Using~\eqref{eq:filled_band_cancellation}, we obtain
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:linear_response_current}
+j^{\rm LR}(t)=\frac1{2\pi^2}\int_{\mathcal B}
+\sum_{n\le N<m}\operatorname{Re}z_{nm,k}
+\frac{\sin(t\Delta_{nm,k})}{\Delta_{nm,k}^2}\,dk.
+\end{equation}
+The preceding decompositions are legitimate:
+$|\sin(t\Delta)|/\Delta^2\le t/\Delta$ and
+$|1-\cos(t\Delta)|/\Delta^2\le t^2/2$, so every term is integrable
+by~\eqref{eq:transition_bound} and the conical gap bound.
+
+\subsection{Time averaging and reduction to two bands}
+\label{sec:dirac_reduc}
